---
import Presentation from "../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `questions = [
    "What planet is known as the Red Planet?",
    "How many bits are in a byte?",
    "What language have we been learning?",
]

answers = [
    "mars",
    "8",
    "python",
]`;
const Figure2 = `for i in range(len(questions)):
    print(questions[i])
    print(answers[i])`;
const Figure3 = `What planet is known as the Red Planet?
mars
How many bits are in a byte?
8
What language have we been learning?
python`;
const Task1 = `questions = [
    "What planet is known as the Red Planet?",
    "How many bits are in a byte?",
    "What language have we been learning?",
]

answers = [
    "mars",
    "8",
    "python",
]
`;
const Task2 = `questions = [
    "What planet is known as the Red Planet?",
    "How many bits are in a byte?",
    "What language have we been learning?",
]

answers = [
    "mars",
    "8",
    "python",
]

score = 0

for i in range(len(questions)):
    response = input(questions[i] + " ").lower()

    if response == answers[i]:
        print("Correct!")
        score = score + 1
    else:
        print(f"Incorrect. The correct answer was {answers[i]}.")
`;
const Task3 = `questions = [
    "What planet is known as the Red Planet?",
    "How many bits are in a byte?",
    "What language have we been learning?",
]

answers = [
    "mars",
    "8",
    "python",
]

score = 0

for i in range(len(questions)):
    response = input(questions[i] + " ").lower()

    if response == answers[i]:
        print("Correct!")
        score = score + 1
    else:
        print(f"Incorrect. The correct answer was {answers[i]}.")

print(f"You scored {score} out of {len(questions)}.")

if score == len(questions):
    print("Perfect score!")
else:
    print("Keep practicing!")
`;
const SampleOutput = `What planet is known as the Red Planet? mars
Correct!
How many bits are in a byte? 8
Correct!
What language have we been learning? javascript
Incorrect. The correct answer was python.
You scored 2 out of 3.
Keep practicing!`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/foce/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.23: Parallel Lists</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 1.23</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Parallel Lists</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Parallel Lists</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Lists, Same Positions</h2>
              <p>
                <b class="text-sn-theme">Parallel lists</b> are two separate
                lists where the same index refers to related data in both
                &mdash; here, <code>questions[i]</code> and{" "}
                <code>answers[i]</code> always go together.
              </p>
              <Code code={Figure1} frame="code" lang="python" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Walking Both Lists Together</h2>
              <p>
                This only works with index-based traversal &mdash;{" "}
                <code>for i in range(len(questions)):</code> &mdash; since
                you need the same <code>i</code> for both lists.
              </p>
              <Code code={Figure2} frame="code" lang="python" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Walking Both Lists Together</h2>
              <Code code={Figure3} frame="terminal" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Today's Project: Trivia Game</h2>
              <p>
                Ask each question, compare the player's answer to the
                matching entry in <code>answers</code>, and keep score
                &mdash; combining everything from this unit into one
                program.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Keeping two lists lined up by index</li>
                <li>Comparing a player's answer to a parallel list</li>
                <li>Tracking a score with the accumulator pattern</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Parallel Lists</dt>
                  <dd>Two or more lists where the same index refers to related values across all of them.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new Python script named <b class="text-sn-theme">1-23-trivia-game.py</b>.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Set Up the Trivia Data</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Create a <code>questions</code> list and a parallel <code>answers</code> list &mdash; keep them the same length.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="python" title="1-23-trivia-game.py" mark={{ range: "1-10" }} />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Ask Each Question</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Loop through both lists by index, comparing the player's answer to the matching entry in <code>answers</code>, and track a score.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task2} frame="code" lang="python" title="1-23-trivia-game.py" mark={{ range: "12-19" }} ins={{ range: "12-19" }} />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Report the Final Score</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>After the loop, print the score out of the total number of questions, with a perfect-score message.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task3} frame="code" lang="python" title="1-23-trivia-game.py" mark={{ range: "21-24" }} ins={{ range: "21-24" }} />
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Verify your program works correctly.</p>

          <Code
            code={SampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            title="Example Output"
          />
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Why must <code>questions</code> and <code>answers</code> stay the same length for this program to work?</li>
            <li>Why does this activity use <code>for i in range(len(questions)):</code> instead of <code>for question in questions:</code>?</li>
            <li>Which earlier activity's accumulator pattern did the score tracker reuse?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
